aid you were in red square i mean i  when i was in beijing three or four  years ago visiting a family member who  was  studying there uh you suddenly struck  that  the airport at beijing is just much  smarter than jfk  that uh you know that as we were walking  down the road she was able to buy  food with her phone just by holding it  up to a street store you can't do  you couldn't do that in the west at the  moment so you know they were ahead on  certain technologies  and on 5g which has been the big thing  where  america has really essentially twisted  britain's arm not to take  chinese 5g technology one of their  problems is there isn't a us 5g at the  moment  so china china is ahead on many key  technologies  james one of the things i was struck by  in your article was though that you you  thought  that china may have um played its cards  too quickly  moved too soon uh  so that it may indeed have undermined  its own deal that it wants to have with  the eu  or on investment that it's it's cracked  down in hong kong  before it's got all its ducks in a row  the same with the uyghurs and the uh  essentially labor camps concentration  camps pretty close  that that it it's simply overplayed  and has given america and its allies  some time to get their act together  james yeah i think as as gideon was  saying  to you earlier andrew they have  miscalculated in that they are making  things very difficult for the west to  ignore i mean since  she came to power they have become more  assertive and especially since  covid they have become much more  aggressive and i think if you look at  their response to these sanctions i  think you see how this nationalist  impulse to always hit back harder  which president xi particularly  encourages this kind of so-called wolf  warrior diplomacy  is flawed because the issue on which the  west finds it  easiest to unify around china isn't the  economic questions that you and gideon  were talking about but it  is obviously the question of 